  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1048 AM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nA TUTT feature will move further eastward and away form the state \nby mid week, while a sfc frontal boundary moves across the SE \nCONUS. Models also agree in keeping the W Atl ridge axis lingering\naround the SE Atl Seaboard, just north of the state through the \nlong term. This will keep the prevailing E/SE flow in place across\nSoFlo, with deep-layered moisture advection persisting during the\nlong term.\n\nModel PWATs keep values above seasonal normals, mainly around or \nabove 2\" through the rest of the week, which will continue to \nsupport scattered to numerous showers and storms each afternoon. \nBest chances for strong convection will again happen with the onset \nof the East coast sea breeze, first along the Atlantic metro areas \nearly in the afternoon, then pushing inland during the late \nafternoon hours. Hazards will include gusty winds, heavy rainfall \nand frequent lightning.\n\nTemperatures are expected to remain seasonably hot each day, with \nafternoon highs in the low to mid 90s. Overnight lows should remain \nin the low 80s across the coastlines, and mid 70s over the\ninterior.",
